  "lessons": "- **Indigenous, Canadian regional, and community life**: Strongly favored: First Nations advocacy and reconciliation, land stewardship, and real community news from BC (Cariboo, Kootenay, Kamloops) and Saskatchewan, including local parades, festivals, and civic events. Sports coverage and routine local listings are rejected.\n- **Environmental framing**: Accepted when the angle is solutions, protection, policy stakes, or industry pivots (sustainable farming, resource-appeals reform, EV strategy resets). Pure disaster coverage (wildfire smoke, tolls, heat, drought headlines) is mostly rejected, though water-scarcity and climate-consequence stories with clear stakes have landed well.\n- **EV market stories**: Rejected when they track ownership decline, sales drops, or model cancellations; accepted when they involve automaker strategy or corporate turnaround.\n- **Science & nature discovery**: Strongly favored: surprising natural phenomena, animal and evolutionary biology, astronomy and night-sky guides, geoscience and critical minerals, and novel-tech research. Wellness pseudoscience and symptom-driven health advice are rejected; health findings tied to environment or brain research can land.\n- **AI & tech**: Accepted for security vulnerabilities, attacks on AI infrastructure, copyright and legal rulings with substance, expert warnings about societal impact, and critiques of bubbles or government overreach. Launches, feature updates, model hype, funding deals, and government appointments are rejected or only marginally interesting.\n- **Practical tech and gadgets**: Privacy and security how-tos that protect users are valued; deals, product roundups, sales, and benchmark or device-refresh news are rejected. Simple consumer app tips (e.g., social",
